.TH QUADRF-APP 8 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-app \- start and stop registered QuadRF applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-app
.B status
.br
.B quadrf-app
.B start
.I app
.br
.B quadrf-app
.B stop
.I app
.br
.B quadrf-app
.B register
.BI --id\  id
.BI --name\  name
.BI --exec\  path
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B quadrf-app
.B unregister
.I id
.SH DESCRIPTION
Starts, stops, registers, and unregisters applications on the operator desktop and web control page.
Packages or manual registrations install a JSON descriptor in
.IR /usr/share/quadrf/apps.d/ .
The visible name, comment and icon come from the application's
.I .desktop
file under
.IR /usr/share/applications/ .
.PP
The
.B register
command configures a custom application in one step: creates the
.I .desktop
launcher, creates a systemd service unit configured for the remote desktop session, writes the catalog descriptor, and updates desktop launchers.
.PP
The
.B unregister
command stops the application if running, removes the service unit, desktop entry, and catalog descriptor, and cleans up desktop launchers.
.PP
The radio front-end is single-client, so
.B start
stops every other exclusive app, waits for the device nodes to be released,
then starts the requested unit.
.PP
.B status
prints JSON with the installed and running state of each app. The web control
panel calls this command through sudoers.
.PP
The included radio apps use the ids
.BR ar
(Spatial RF Vision, WebSocket feed for
.IR /AR/ ),
.BR ntsc
(Camera Decoder),
.BR psd
(PSD Plot) and
.BR nearfield
(Near-Field Phasors). A copy started from the remote desktop is stopped as well, so it
does not keep the radio locked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf (1),
.BR desktop-file-validate (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR quadrf-rf-vision (1),
.BR quadrf-ntsc (1),
.BR quadrf-psd (1),
.BR quadrf-nearfield (1)
